The animals gathered where the pine roots entered the dark. Bear shook the trunk until needles fell like dull stars. Raven croaked charms into the bark. Nothing opened. Then Tortoise came, carrying moss, patience, and one clear raindrop in her shell.

“I will go below,” she said.

Squirrel chattered, “The deep is far, and your feet are slow.”

Tortoise answered, “Slow feet hear the earth’s directions.”

Down she climbed through root halls, pebble gates, and clay chambers. For seven nights she followed a small green glimmer. At last she found the seed, cold and bright, but too dry to wake. Tortoise tipped her shell and gave it the raindrop.

The seed cracked. A stem of light rushed upward through the pine. When it touched the sky, dawn blossomed. Red filled berries, green clothed needles, blue entered lakes, gold warmed fox fur, and violet settled in twilight.

Tortoise kept only the seed’s dark dust on her shell, so all remember patience can carry morning upward.
